---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PasswordsDemo from '../../components/PasswordsDemo.astro';
const repository = 'https://github.com/roywasker/My_Passwords';
const decisions = [
  { number: '01', title: 'Make locking a session boundary.', tag: 'LIFECYCLE · COROUTINES', description: 'VaultSession owns the open database, the in-memory vault key and authentication state. Locking advances a session generation, revokes access and clears its owned key buffer. Operations check their session ticket so work from an earlier session can be cancelled.', benefit: 'Screens and repositories share a concrete boundary for access. The Android host also locks when it leaves the foreground, except during a configuration change.', tradeoff: 'Asynchronous operations must consistently respect that boundary. Clearing owned byte arrays does not guarantee that every temporary copy or JVM string has been erased.', file: 'core/src/main/java/com/example/core/auth/VaultSession.kt', source: 'Read the session owner' },
  { number: '02', title: 'Treat key lifecycle as part of storage.', tag: 'ANDROID KEYSTORE · AES-GCM · SQLCIPHER', description: 'The app combines a SQLCipher-backed Room database with AES-GCM encryption for credential fields. Android Keystore provides an authentication-bound key for wrapping the vault key; biometric enrollment changes invalidate that Keystore key.', benefit: 'Storage, field encryption and authenticated key access have separate responsibilities. The database owns a copy of its passphrase and clears that buffer when it closes.', tradeoff: 'Layered encryption adds recovery and invalidation paths to maintain. Recovery must be configured in advance and works within the same installation; it is not a portable backup or cloud sync.', file: 'core/src/main/java/com/example/core/crypto/CryptoManager.kt', source: 'Read the cryptographic implementation' },
  { number: '03', title: 'Make a password change an atomic operation.', tag: 'ROOM TRANSACTIONS · HISTORY', description: 'The DAO archives the previous encrypted password and updates the credential in one transaction. It checks that the stored credential still matches the expected values before writing. History restoration checks ownership and archives the current password before restoring the selected version.', benefit: 'A successful password update keeps its corresponding history. Separate trash and restore actions let people reverse an accidental deletion without immediately losing the record.', tradeoff: 'History retains more encrypted data and needs a retention policy. The app’s 30-day trash cleanup runs when the vault or trash is opened, rather than promising deletion at an exact background deadline.', file: 'core/src/main/java/com/example/core/data/dao/CredentialDao.kt', source: 'Read the history transactions' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="MyPasswords — Android engineering case study | Roy Wasker" description="Inside MyPasswords: a local Android password vault, an interactive Hebrew UI demo, and three engineering decisions about session access, encrypted storage and history.">
  <article class="case-study" id="top">
    <header class="case-hero section-shell">
      <a class="back-link" href="/#work">← Back to selected work</a>
      <p class="eyebrow">CASE STUDY / 02 <span>MYPASSWORDS · ANDROID</span></p>
      <h1>Your vault.<br /><em>On your device.</em></h1>
      <p class="case-lead">Passwords are personal. MyPasswords brings them into a local Android vault with biometric access, a Hebrew interface and everyday tools for finding, organizing and recovering credentials.</p>
      <div class="case-actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="#demo">Explore the vault demo <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><a class="source-link" href={repository} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Explore source code ↗</a></div>
      <dl class="project-facts"><div><dt>Product</dt><dd>Local password vault</dd></div><div><dt>Platform</dt><dd>Native Android</dd></div><div><dt>Interface</dt><dd>Jetpack Compose · Hebrew RTL</dd></div><div><dt>Storage</dt><dd>Room · SQLCipher · AES-GCM</dd></div></dl>
    </header>
    <section class="case-section section-shell story-grid" aria-labelledby="challenge-title">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">THE CHALLENGE</p><h2 id="challenge-title">Keep everyday access simple.<br /><em>Make its boundaries explicit.</em></h2></div>
      <div class="story-copy"><p>Finding a login should be straightforward. Underneath that familiar list, a vault has harder questions to answer: when is a session open, what happens when the app leaves the foreground, and how can a password change be reversed?</p><p>MyPasswords addresses those questions with a central session owner, encrypted local storage and transactional history. The Compose interface adds search, categories, favorites, password health checks and a recycle bin.</p><p>The Android app works locally without an Internet permission. This case study describes code and tradeoffs in the repository; it does not claim an independent security audit or measured production outcomes.</p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="demo-section" id="demo" aria-labelledby="demo-title">
      <div class="section-shell demo-layout"><div class="demo-intro"><p class="eyebrow">TOUCH THE EXPERIENCE</p><h2 id="demo-title">Open. Find.<br /><em>Make it yours.</em></h2><p>Explore a recreation of the app’s Hebrew interface, using its navy surfaces, teal accents and right-to-left layout.</p><ol><li><strong>Open the demo</strong><span>Step through a clearly labeled simulation of the unlock flow.</span></li><li><strong>Find an account</strong><span>Search sample records, filter categories or view favorites.</span></li><li><strong>Manage the list</strong><span>Add a preset account, reveal a sample password, or delete and restore a record.</span></li></ol><p class="demo-context">This browser demo uses fictional accounts held in memory. It does not run Android, authenticate biometrics or encrypt stored passwords. Refresh or reset to start again. The phone keeps the same dimensions as you change screens.</p></div><PasswordsDemo /></div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section section-shell" id="decisions" aria-labelledby="decisions-title"><p class="eyebrow">BEHIND THE INTERFACE</p><h2 id="decisions-title">Three decisions.<br /><em>The reasoning behind the build.</em></h2>
      <div class="decisions">{decisions.map(decision => <article class="decision"><span class="decision-number" aria-hidden="true">{decision.number}</span><div><p class="decision-tag">{decision.tag}</p><h3>{decision.title}</h3><p>{decision.description}</p><div class="decision-detail"><div><h4>What this enables</h4><p>{decision.benefit}</p></div><div><h4>The tradeoff</h4><p>{decision.tradeoff}</p></div></div><a class="source-link" href={`${repository}/blob/HEAD/${decision.file}`} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">{decision.source} ↗</a></div></article>)}</div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section section-shell" id="screenshots" aria-labelledby="screenshots-title"><p class="eyebrow">FROM THE APP</p><h2 id="screenshots-title">The original Compose interface.</h2><p class="section-description">UI renders from the Android project’s local Robolectric tests, using synthetic accounts. These show the app’s Compose screens, separate from the browser recreation above.</p><div class="screenshots">{[['welcome', 'A welcoming first step'], ['passwords', 'Accounts at a glance'], ['add-password', 'Adding a credential']].map(([file, caption]) => <figure><img src={`/images/my-passwords/${file}.png`} alt={`MyPasswords Hebrew Android interface: ${caption.toLowerCase()}`} width="411" height="891" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>{caption}</figcaption></figure>)}</div></section>
    <section class="case-section section-shell story-grid" aria-labelledby="next-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">WHAT I WOULD VERIFY NEXT</p><h2 id="next-title">Confidence comes<br /><em>from the edge cases.</em></h2></div><div class="story-copy"><p>A useful next verification pass would exercise backgrounding during a write, biometric enrollment changes, and recovery after key invalidation on physical devices.</p><p>I would also review the Hebrew flows with TalkBack: finding a record, revealing a password and restoring from trash should each have a clear focus order and announcement. These are proposed verification priorities, not claims that this case study has completed a device or security audit.</p><a class="source-link" href={repository} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the MyPasswords repository ↗</a></div></section>
    <footer class="case-footer section-shell"><div><p class="eyebrow">LET’S BUILD SOMETHING USEFUL</p><h2>Have a mobile challenge?</h2></div><a class="button button-primary" href="/#contact">Let’s talk <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="back-link" href="/#work">← All projects</a></footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
